    "summaryAr": "About Ramp Ramp is building the smart infrastructure for finance teams, embedded in the transaction flow of every dollar a business spends. We automate how over $275B in annualized spend flows in and out of 85,000+ companies: authorizing payments, flagging risk, categorizing spend, and closing books. The problems are high-stakes, data-dense, and unforgiving. We hire people with high agency and high urgency. We look f",
    "descriptionAr": "About Ramp Ramp is building the smart infrastructure for finance teams, embedded in the transaction flow of every dollar a business spends. We automate how over $275B in annualized spend flows in and out of 85,000+ companies: authorizing payments, flagging risk, categorizing spend, and closing books. The problems are high-stakes, data-dense, and unforgiving. We hire people with high agency and high urgency. We look for slope over intercept. We care less about where you trained and more about what you’ve built. At Ramp, everyone is a builder who owns problems end to end and makes consequential decisions that shape the outcome. The median Ramp customer saves 7.5% and grows revenue 16% in their first year – far in excess of businesses operating without Ramp. We believe every ambitious company deserves the same. If you want to build systems that directly shape how companies move and manage billions, Ramp is the place to do it. About the Role Ramp is hiring a People Strategy, M&A Lead to build a function that doesn't exist here today: how Ramp attracts, evaluates, prices, and closes the teams and companies that join us through an acquisition. This is a new role, and you'll build it from the ground up. You'll own that work end to end. For team acquisitions, that means the pipeline, the assessment, the economics, the founder and team conversations, the offers, and post-close integration with People Operations and the hiring teams. When Ramp acquires a company outright, you'll own the people side of the deal alongside Corporate Development: who stays, at what level, on what terms, and how we communicate it. You'll report to Ramp's head of People Finance & Strategy and work day to day with Corporate Development, Legal, and the business leaders who'll take the teams. This is a senior individual contributor role with direct exposure to our co-founders, CFO, and head of Corporate Development. You'll run the hiring process yourself, from first conversation to signed offers, and stay with the team through post-close integration, which no one owns at Ramp today. The scope will change as we do more of this, and defining it is part of the job. This role will grow with what Ramp does. As volume grows, so does the scope: larger acquisitions alongside Corporate Development, the people side of company acquisitions, and strategy work across People Finance & Strategy. At Ramp, people who have built deep operating judgment in roles like this have gone on to lead major business areas. For the right person, this is a way to build deal judgment and talent judgment at the same time, with the company context to take on broader operating roles over time in corporate development, general management, or People leadership. You don't need to have done this exact job before. You do need to have run deals or searches yourself and be credible with founders and with executives. What You'll Do Own the relationships. The founder, every member of the team, and the Ramp leaders who'd take them hear from you first, and you keep those conversations moving from the first call to signed offers. Assess the team with the leaders who'd take it: who's essential, what roles and levels they'd hold at Ramp, and what it would take for them to join and stay. Price and structure the package. Use market data and our compensation framework to build an offer for each person and a budget for the team, including salary, equity, sign-on, and retention. Write the recommendation. Pursue, pass, or route to Corporate Development, with the economics compared to hiring the same people individually. Present it to our co-founders, CFO, and head of Corporate Development. Run the process: interviews, references, light diligence, negotiation within agreed bounds, and close. Build the pipeline with Corporate Development. Keep a current view of the companies and teams in the areas Ramp is building, and of the investors, founders, and bankers who see them early. Qualify quickly and pass quickly. Handle the mechanics with Legal and People Operations: confidentiality, non-solicits, immigration, the team's existing equity, and timing. Own post-close integration with People Operations and the hiring teams: start dates, managers, onboarding, retention checkpoints. Track whether the people we set out to hire joined, stayed, and performed, and turn what you learn into a playbook. On company acquisitions, own the people workstream with Corporate Development: team diligence, leveling, retention design, offers, and communications. What You Need 6-10 years in venture capital, growth equity, corporate development, M&A advisory, management consulting, or executive search, including deals, searches, or client work you ran yourself. Intellectual curiosity about Ramp and the space we operate in. You want to understand the business well enough to know which teams would make it better and why. Extremely strong judgment. You can tell a strong team from a good pitch, and you know when to push, when to pass, and when to bring in Corporate Development or Legal. Sourcing experience. You've found companies or people before they were introduced to you, and you can get a founder to take a call. High emotional intelligence. You build trust quickly with founders and senior engineers, and you can deliver a hard answer without losing the relationship. Comfort with executives. You can present a recommendation to a CEO with the full breakdown, handle pushback, and change your view when the facts change. Financial fluency: cap tables, preference stacks, options and RSUs, and what each is worth to the person holding it. Negotiation experience. The hard conversations in this job get solved with creativity, judgment, and structure, and you've done that yourself. Integration experience. You've brought a team or company into a new organization and seen it through to people being productive. Calm and structure. You can run several live opportunities at once and keep every thread moving. Ambition and ",
